Implemented one Stage 1 slice in [src/dual_plane/rt.rs#L620](/Users/robbym/go/src/github.com/robmorgan/timestretch-rs/src/dual_plane/rt.rs#L620) and [src/dual_plane/rt.rs#L2673](/Users/robbym/go/src/github.com/robmorgan/timestretch-rs/src/dual_plane/rt.rs#L2673): if ratio-motion freeze re-arms while auto mode is already on `Scratch`, the RT path now cancels any stale pending return-to-`Mix` profile/tier transition and snaps lane weights back to the current scratch tier instead of leaving the old retarget alive. I added a focused regression test for that exact stale-transition case. `ROADMAP.md` was left unchanged because Stage 1 was already `[~]`, and I did not mark it `[x]`.

- Files changed: [src/dual_plane/rt.rs](/Users/robbym/go/src/github.com/robmorgan/timestretch-rs/src/dual_plane/rt.rs)
- Checks run: `cargo fmt --all --check`; `cargo test ratio_motion_freeze_ -- --nocapture`; `cargo test --features qa-harnesses --release --test quality_gates quality_gate_dual_plane_fast_modulation_artifacts -- --nocapture`
- Remaining risk: the fast-modulation release gate still reports the same rare outlier (`modulated max/p99 = 63.591`), so this slice hardens stale scratch-hold retarget cancellation but does not eliminate the remaining rare boundary-spike pathology.
